+# The Daily Grind
+
+A coffee homepage with a server-rendered rewards registration form and browser-assisted email ownership verification. Requires Node.js 24+ and pnpm.
+
+## Commands
+
+- `pnpm install --frozen-lockfile`
+- `pnpm test` — isolated request-handler and cryptographic verification tests; no listening server or real email provider required.
+- `pnpm build` — syntax-checks source and copies runtime files into `dist/`; does not start or import the server entrypoint.
+- `pnpm start` — production-capable Node HTTP entrypoint, normally behind an HTTPS reverse proxy.
+
+## Runtime configuration
+
+Set environment variables in your hosting environment (see `.env.example`; files are not automatically loaded):
+
+- `PUBLIC_ORIGIN`: exact public HTTPS origin used as the verification audience and expected POST Origin. Mandatory with `NODE_ENV=production`. Never inferred from Host or forwarded headers.
+- `EMAIL_VERIFICATION_ORIGIN_TRIAL_TOKEN`: valid Email Verification origin-trial token registered for the public origin. Returned in the homepage's `Origin-Trial` response header. No placeholder token is used at runtime.
+- `DATA_DIR`: writable, persistent directory for SQLite (default `./data`). Keep private and back up with normal SQLite procedures; never serve it as static content.
+- `PORT`: HTTP listening port (default `11222`).
+
+Use a host that supports a long-running Node process and persistent storage. A static-only server cannot run the verification endpoint. The build output can run with `node dist/server.mjs` after installing production dependencies. Bind a single instance to a persistent SQLite database; session challenges and rate limits are stored alongside accounts. The basic rate limiter uses the direct socket address and intentionally does not trust forwarded headers, so a reverse proxy may share an attempt budget across customers; configure a trusted-edge rate limiter before scaling.
+
+## Verification flow
+
+`GET /` creates a random 256-bit challenge and anti-CSRF token in server-side SQLite state. The session cookie is HttpOnly, SameSite=Lax, and Secure on HTTPS. The hidden `autocomplete="email-verification-token"` control receives a fresh nonce in the same native POST form as `autocomplete="email"`. Pages are served with `Cache-Control: no-store`.
+
+`POST /api/verify-email` atomically consumes the session challenge, checks request origin, CSRF token, body size, input constraints, and attempt budget. It then verifies:
+
+1. Email-domain DNS TXT delegation to the claimed HTTPS issuer.
+2. Issuer discovery metadata and public JWKS, with private-network addresses and redirects rejected.
+3. Issuer JWT signature, type, issuer, algorithm and freshness.
+4. Browser holder-key signature, audience, nonce, timestamp and SD-JWT hash binding.
+5. Verified email claim and case-insensitive equality with the submitted address, including selectively disclosed claims.
+
+Only successful verification creates an account with a unique email key and scrypt password hash. No raw password or verification token is saved or logged. Duplicate accounts cannot overwrite an existing account. The sample implements registration; returning-user sign-in, password recovery and points/redemption are separate features.
+
+Email Verification is an experimental browser/provider capability, currently requiring an origin trial. A valid deployment token, supported browser and participating email provider with an active customer session are external prerequisites. See [Chrome's relying-party documentation](https://developer.chrome.com/docs/identity/email-verification/verifier).
+
+Per the requirement to send **no OTP or magic link**, absent or invalid tokens show an accessible retry explanation, preserve non-sensitive form values, and create no account. There is deliberately no email-sending fallback and no claim that email syntax validation proves ownership. Verification cannot be completed by an unsupported browser. No browser API is simulated.
+
+## Checks performed
+
+Automated tests cover genuine issuer/browser signatures, missing-key-id rotation, selective disclosures, email mismatch, false verification claims, wrong nonce/audience/hash/key, expired tokens, discovery mismatch, private-address rejection, secure cookies, CSRF, replay, unsupported browsers, escaped form values, password hashing and durable account creation. Manual browser verification and server startup were not performed.
